            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atomic Foundations of Matter"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 165"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.088,
            0.662,
            0.122
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9. As in experimental set-up 1, a brisk effervescence occurs, which inflates the balloon during the reaction."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.123,
            0.464,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "10. Record the final reading (Fig. 9.3c)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.141,
            0.641,
            0.159
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11. Are the initial and the final readings same in this case?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.161,
            0.664,
            0.299
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In this experiment, the final reading matches the initial reading. You have noticed that the total mass of vinegar and baking soda (sodium hydrogencarbonate) before the chemical reaction is equal to the total mass of carbon dioxide and other substances formed after the reaction. In experimental set-up 1, the mass difference occurs because the gas produced by the chemical reaction escapes, resulting in a difference between the initial and the final readings."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.31,
            0.525,
            0.331
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9.1 Law of Conservation of Mass"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.682,
            0.126,
            0.963,
            0.339
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.682,
                0.126,
                0.963,
                0.339
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_image_7.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.334,
            0.664,
            0.419
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Activity 9.2 demonstrates that the total mass remains the same before and after a chemical reaction. So, matter can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction. This is known as the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Law of Conservation of Mass",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", proposed by Antoine Lavoisier in 1789."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.684,
            0.344,
            0.901,
            0.373
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 9.3: (c) Weight of the final reaction mixture"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.424,
            0.304,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.441,
            0.248,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.12,
                0.441,
                0.248,
                0.553
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.252,
            0.444,
            0.622,
            0.554
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Antoine Lavoisier",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is known as the Father of Modern Chemistry. He proposed the Law of Conservation of Mass. This law applies to every chemical reaction. Lavoisier continued to study this and proposed that “…in every operation an equal quantity of matter exists both before and after the operation”."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.569,
            0.662,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us consider the reaction between sodium sulfate and barium chloride to further verify this law."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.679,
            0.442,
            0.962,
            0.629
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 14,
              "bbox": [
                0.679,
                0.442,
                0.962,
                0.629
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_image_14.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.612,
            0.628,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 9.3: Let us verify the law — Group activity",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.634,
            0.662,
            0.668
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Place two clean and dry 100 mL conical flasks on a weighing balance, and mark them A and B."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.682,
            0.634,
            0.907,
            0.664
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 9.4: (a) Weight of solutions (reactants) before mixing"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.669,
            0.662,
            0.703
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Set the balance reading to zero by pressing the tare or reset button."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.704,
            0.662,
            0.738
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Pour about 10 mL of 1 % m/v sodium sulfate solution into the Conical Flask marked A."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.739,
            0.662,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. In the Conical Flask B, pour about 10 mL of 1 % m/v barium chloride solution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.773,
            0.662,
            0.824
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Leave both Conical Flasks A and B on the weighing balance undisturbed, and record the total mass of both the solutions (Fig. 9.4a)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.825,
            0.662,
            0.86
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Transfer the solution from Conical Flask B to Conical Flask A and mix the two solutions carefully."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.123,
            0.86,
            0.355,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. What do you observe?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.878,
            0.662,
            0.913
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. Now, place both the Conical Flasks A and B on the weighing balance again as shown in Fig. 9.4b, and note the reading."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.913,
            0.662,
            0.947
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9. Do you observe any change in the reading after mixing the solutions?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 26,
          "bbox": [
            0.681,
            0.674,
            0.965,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
